<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a 3-4-5 Triangle?</h2>
  <p>A 3-4-5 triangle is named for its three side lengths.</p>
  <p>It is a right triangle whose side lengths follow the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[3 : 4 : 5]]')} />. The longest side, 5, is the hypotenuse, and it sits opposite the 90-degree angle. The other two sides, 3 and 4, are the legs, and they meet at the right angle. In triangle ABC, angle C is 90°, side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the shorter leg of length 3, side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the longer leg of length 4, and side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse of length 5.</p>
  <p>It is called a Pythagorean triple because 3, 4, and 5 are whole numbers that satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. That whole-number property makes the triangle useful: builders, teachers, and students can check a right angle with ordinary whole-number measurements, without square roots or decimals. It is the smallest Pythagorean triple, and every scaled copy, such as 6-8-10, is also one.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-main.svg')} alt="Labeled 3-4-5 right triangle ABC with shorter leg a equals 3, longer leg b equals 4, hypotenuse c equals 5, and a right-angle marker" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC: legs a = 3 and b = 4 meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse is c = 5.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-pythagorean -->
<section id="sec-pythagorean" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is 3-4-5 a Pythagorean Triple?</h2>
  <p>The numbers 3, 4, and 5 satisfy the Pythagorean theorem exactly.</p>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the legs:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the 3-4-5 side lengths:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Work out each square:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[9 + 16 = 25]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both sides equal 25, so the equation holds. A Pythagorean triple is a set of three whole numbers that satisfies this equation, and 3-4-5 is the smallest one. The equality also works in reverse: if a triangle has side lengths 3, 4, and 5, the angle opposite the side of length 5 must be 90°.</p>
  <p>The side of length 5 must be the hypotenuse because it is the longest side. The sides of length 3 and 4 must be the legs, because they meet at the right angle. The <a href="/pythagorean-triples-calculator/">Pythagorean triples calculator</a> lists other whole-number triples, including 5-12-13, a different triple.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-pythagoras.svg')} alt="Pythagorean diagram for the 3-4-5 triangle showing 3 squared plus 4 squared equals 5 squared, with 9 plus 16 equals 25" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">3² + 4² = 5², since 9 + 16 = 25, so 3-4-5 is a Pythagorean triple.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-sides -->
<section id="sec-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Three Sides of a 3-4-5 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse sits across from it.</p>
  <p>The shorter leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> has length 3 in the basic triangle. It is side BC in triangle ABC, and it sits opposite angle A, about 36.87°. The longer leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> has length 4, is side AC, and sits opposite angle B, about 53.13°. The two legs meet at vertex C, where they form the 90° angle.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> has length 5, is side AB, and sits opposite the 90° angle. It is the longest side, because the largest angle always sits opposite the longest side. When the triangle is scaled, the roles never change: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> stays the shorter leg, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> stays the longer leg, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> stays the hypotenuse.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Opposite angle</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Length</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Shorter leg a (BC)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">36.87°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Longer leg b (AC)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">53.13°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">4</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse c (AB)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5, longest side</td></tr>
