  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-ratio -->
<section id="sec-ratio" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the 3-4-5 Side Ratio</h2>
  <p>The ratio 3:4:5 fixes the shape of every 3-4-5 triangle.</p>
  <p>The side ratio of a 3-4-5 triangle is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a : b : c = 3 : 4 : 5]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Each part of the ratio describes one side. The shorter leg represents 3 proportional units, the longer leg represents 4 proportional units, and the hypotenuse represents 5 proportional units. Written with the scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, the three sides are <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3k]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4k]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5k]]')} />, so the ratio in scaled form is <span set:html={rich('[[3k : 4k : 5k]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The ratio does not change when the triangle is enlarged or reduced, because multiplying all three sides by the same number leaves their proportions untouched. The sets 3, 4, 5 and 6, 8, 10 and 9, 12, 15 and 15, 20, 25 all represent the same triangle shape at different sizes.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-scaled.svg')} alt="Three similar 3-4-5 triangles at different sizes labeled 3-4-5, 6-8-10, and 9-12-15 with the same proportions" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">3-4-5, 6-8-10, and 9-12-15 have the same shape and the same 3:4:5 ratio.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-scale-factor -->
<section id="sec-scale-factor" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Scale Factor in a 3-4-5 Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The scale factor multiplies every side by the same number.</p>
  <p>A scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> multiplies all three original side lengths by the same positive number. The scaled sides are:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 3k, \\quad b = 4k, \\quad c = 5k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1]]')} /> you get the original triangle, 3, 4, 5. A value of <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> greater than 1 enlarges it, and a value between 0 and 1 reduces it, for example <span set:html={rich('[[k = 0.5]]')} /> gives 1.5, 2, 2.5. The scale factor must be positive: zero gives no triangle, and a negative value cannot give valid side lengths.</p>
  <p>The scale factor changes size, not shape. All corresponding side lengths stay proportional, the angles stay the same, and the ratio stays 3:4:5. The table above shows how the sides, area, and perimeter change with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />: the perimeter grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> while the area grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> squared.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scale factor k</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Sides</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Area</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Perimeter</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">0.5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1.5, 2, 2.5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1.5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">6</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3, 4, 5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">6</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">12</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">6, 8, 10</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">24</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">24</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">9, 12, 15</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">54</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">36</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-scale-factor.svg')} alt="Scaled 3-4-5 triangle with scale factor k equals 2, labeled sides a equals 6, b equals 8, and c equals 10" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">With k = 2, the sides are a = 6, b = 8, and c = 10.</figcaption>
