</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-leg-a -->
<section id="sec-leg-a" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Calculate the First Leg</h2>
  <p>The shorter leg is three times the scale factor.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 3k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the shorter leg and <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is the scale factor. The formula is a direct reading of the ratio: the shorter leg represents 3 units for every unit of <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so multiplying 3 by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> gives the scaled length. Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 3 \\times 4]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = 12]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is a scaled side length, not an angle and not a scale factor. It is always shorter than the other two sides, because 3 is the smallest part of the 3:4:5 ratio, and it stays the shorter leg at every positive scale factor.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-leg-b -->
<section id="sec-leg-b" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Calculate the Second Leg</h2>
  <p>The longer leg is four times the scale factor.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 4k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the longer leg and <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is the scale factor. The longer leg represents 4 units for every unit of <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so multiplying 4 by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> gives the scaled length. Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 4 \\times 4]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = 16]]]')} /></p>
  <p>This side stays longer than the first leg at every positive scale factor, because 4 is larger than 3 and both sides are multiplied by the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. It also stays shorter than the hypotenuse, because 4 is smaller than 5. Keep the two legs in the right order: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the 3 side and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the 4 side.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-hypotenuse -->
<section id="sec-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Calculate the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>The hypotenuse is five times the scale factor.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 5k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse and <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is the scale factor. The hypotenuse represents 5 units for every unit of <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so multiplying 5 by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> gives the scaled length. Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 5 \\times 4]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = 20]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse stays the longest side at every positive scale factor, because 5 is the largest part of the ratio. Check it against the legs: with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 4]]')} /> the sides are 12, 16, and 20, and <span set:html={rich('[[12^2 + 16^2 = 144 + 256 = 400 = 20^2]]')} />, so the Pythagorean theorem confirms the result.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-area -->
<section id="sec-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Scaled 3-4-5 Triangle</h2>
  <p>The area uses the two legs, and the scale factor is squared.</p>
  <p>The area of any right triangle is half the product of its two legs, because the legs act as the base and the perpendicular height:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the scaled legs, <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3k]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4k]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\frac{(3k)(4k)}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Simplify:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = 6k^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Division by two is necessary because a triangle covers half the rectangle formed by its two legs. The factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is squared because both legs carry a factor of <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. Area is measured in square units. Doubling the scale factor quadruples the area: with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> the area is 24 square units, four times the 6 square units at <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1]]')} />. For a general right triangle, use the <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">right triangle area calculator</a> instead.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = 6(3^2)]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = 54 \\text{ square units}]]]')} /></p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-area.svg')} alt="3-4-5 triangle area diagram with base 3k, height 4k, and area 6k squared, showing k equals 2 gives 24 square units" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Area from base 3k and height 4k: A = 6k², and k = 2 gives 24 square units.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-perimeter -->
<section id="sec-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter of a Scaled 3-4-5 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Add all three sides, and the perimeter scales with k.</p>
  <p>The perimeter is the total length around the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = a + b + c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the scaled sides:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 3k + 4k + 5k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Simplify:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 12k]]]')} /></p>
  <p>All three sides must be added, because the perimeter traces the full boundary. Leaving out any side gives a shorter distance that is not the perimeter. Perimeter scales directly with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so doubling <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> doubles the perimeter, unlike the area, which quadruples. Perimeter is in linear units, never square units. For a general right triangle, use the <a href="/right-triangle-perimeter-calculator/">right triangle perimeter calculator</a>.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 12 \\times 3]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[P = 36 \\text{ units}]]]')} /></p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-perimeter.svg')} alt="3-4-5 triangle perimeter diagram showing P equals 3k plus 4k plus 5k equals 12k, with k equals 2 giving 24 units" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Perimeter: P = 3k + 4k + 5k = 12k, and k = 2 gives 24 units.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-find-k -->
<section id="sec-find-k" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Scale Factor From a Known Side</h2>
  <p>Divide a known side by its part of the ratio.</p>
  <p>The calculator accepts <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> directly, so these equations are manual calculations that help you find <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> when you already know one side. Divide the known side by the number that side represents in the 3:4:5 ratio. If the shorter leg is known:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[k = \\frac{a}{3}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>If the longer leg is known:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[k = \\frac{b}{4}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>If the hypotenuse is known:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[k = \\frac{c}{5}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Three short examples: the shorter leg <span set:html={rich('[[a = 12]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[k = 12 / 3 = 4]]')} />; the longer leg <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[k = 20 / 4 = 5]]')} />; the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 30]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[k = 30 / 5 = 6]]')} />. Once you have <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, enter it into the calculator. The calculator itself does not accept a side length and find <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> for you.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Scaling a 3-4-5 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Scale factor k = 2, worked through from start to finish.</p>
  <p>Start with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> and find every measurement of the scaled triangle, one formula at a time. Each step uses a formula from the sections above, and the summary table at the end collects the results.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the first leg: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3k = 3 \\times 2 = 6]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Find the second leg: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4k = 4 \\times 2 = 8]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Find the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5k = 5 \\times 2 = 10]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Find the area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6k^2 = 6 \\times 4 = 24]]')} /> square units.</li>
