</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Different Scale Factors</h2>
  <p>Eight worked examples, from the original triangle to k = 10.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Original Triangle, k = 1</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(1^2) = 6]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(1) = 12]]')} /> units.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Scale Factor of 2</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(2^2) = 24]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(2) = 24]]')} /> units. Doubling <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> doubles the sides and the perimeter, and quadruples the area.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Scale Factor of 3</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(3^2) = 54]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(3) = 36]]')} /> units.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Scale Factor of 5</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 5]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(5^2) = 150]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(5) = 60]]')} /> units. This 15-20-25 triangle is a common size in construction layout.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Scale Factor of 0.5</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 0.5]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 1.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2.5]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(0.5^2) = 1.5]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(0.5) = 6]]')} /> units. A scale factor below 1 shrinks the triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Scale Factor of 1.5</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 1.5]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.5]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(1.5^2) = 13.5]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(1.5) = 18]]')} /> units. Decimal scale factors work exactly like whole-number ones.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Scale Factor of 4</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 4]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 16]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(4^2) = 96]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(4) = 48]]')} /> units.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Scale Factor of 10</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[k = 10]]')} />. Sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 30]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 40]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 50]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6(10^2) = 600]]')} /> square units. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P = 12(10) = 120]]')} /> units. The area has grown by a factor of 100, while the sides have grown by a factor of 10.</p>
  <p>Across all eight examples, the pattern never changes: sides grow with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, perimeter grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, and area grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> squared.</p>
