<!-- Section 16: sec-right-angle -->
<section id="sec-right-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the 3-4-5 Triangle Always Keep a 90-Degree Angle?</h2>
  <p>Scaling never breaks the Pythagorean relationship.</p>
  <p>The scaled sides are <span set:html={rich('[[3k]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4k]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5k]]')} />. Apply the Pythagorean theorem to them:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[(3k)^2 + (4k)^2 = (5k)^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Expand each square:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[9k^2 + 16k^2 = 25k^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both sides equal <span set:html={rich('[[25k^2]]')} />, so the relationship holds for every positive <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. The factor <span set:html={rich('[[k^2]]')} /> appears on both sides, which is why scaling cannot change the angle. The two acute angles stay at about 36.87° and 53.13°, and their sum stays at 90°, so the third angle stays at exactly 90°.</p>
  <p>This is the same idea as similarity: every scaled 3-4-5 triangle is similar to the original, with equal corresponding angles and proportional sides. A triangle with sides 6, 8, 10 has the same angles as one with sides 3, 4, 5.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-construction -->
<section id="sec-construction" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the 3-4-5 Rule to Check a Square Corner</h2>
  <p>Mark 3, measure 4, and check the diagonal is 5.</p>
  <p>Builders use the 3-4-5 method to check a 90-degree corner without a square tool. Start at the corner and mark 3 units along one side, then mark 4 units along the adjoining side. Measure the distance between the two marked endpoints: if it is 5 units, the angle is 90 degrees, subject to measurement accuracy.</p>
  <p>A practical example uses feet: 3 feet along one direction, 4 feet along the adjoining direction, and a diagonal of 5 feet. The check is <span set:html={rich('[[3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2]]')} />, since <span set:html={rich('[[9 + 16 = 25]]')} />. If the diagonal is more than 5 feet, the corner is wider than 90 degrees; if less, it is narrower.</p>
  <p>Larger multiples work better on bigger layouts, because a longer baseline makes a small marking error a smaller share of the measurement. Use 6-8-10, 9-12-15, 12-16-20, or 15-20-25 feet, keeping the same unit on all three measurements.</p>
  <p>The method checks the right-angle relationship when the measurements are accurate. Errors, uneven surfaces, tape sag, and marking inaccuracies can affect the result, so do not claim it guarantees perfect construction accuracy. Keep the tape level and taut, and repeat the check from the other corner where the layout allows it.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-construction.svg')} alt="Construction layout showing a corner with 3 feet marked along one direction, 4 feet along the adjoining direction, and a 5 foot diagonal with a right-angle marker" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Mark 3 feet one way and 4 feet the other way: a 5 foot diagonal means a square corner.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Applications of the 3-4-5 Triangle in Construction</h2>
  <p>The 3-4-5 rule turns up wherever a square corner matters.</p>
  <p>Checking right-angle corners is the main use: a builder marks 3 and 4 units from a corner and checks the 5-unit diagonal before fixing a frame in place. Laying out foundations uses the same check at each corner, so the walls start square.</p>
  <p>Setting out floor plans, checking square room layouts, deck construction, patio installation, and tile layout all rely on square corners, and the method works for each of them at whatever scale fits the job. Fence layout, carpentry, landscaping, and surveying use the same check for right angles.</p>
  <p>Use realistic measurements for the job, such as 3-4-5 feet for a small frame or 9-12-15 feet for a foundation corner. The method is a practical check, not a substitute for professional surveying or construction requirements.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-similar -->
<section id="sec-similar" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Similar Triangles and the 3-4-5 Ratio</h2>
  <p>Every scaled 3-4-5 triangle is similar to the original.</p>
  <p>Similar triangles have the same shape but different sizes: their corresponding angles are equal and their corresponding sides are proportional. The triangles 3-4-5 and 6-8-10 have identical angles, about 36.87°, 53.13°, and 90°, and each side of the second is exactly twice the matching side of the first.</p>
  <p>The scale factor changes the size while the shape stays unchanged. All side ratios stay 3:4:5, so <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 3 : 4 : 5]]')} /> holds for the original and for every scaled copy. That is why the calculator can describe any scaled 3-4-5 triangle with one number, <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-vs-special -->
<section id="sec-vs-special" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between 3-4-5, 45-45-90, and 30-60-90 Triangles</h2>
  <p>Three right triangles, three different side ratios.</p>
  <p>A 3-4-5 triangle is not automatically a 45-45-90 or a 30-60-90 triangle. All three are right triangles, but their angles and side ratios differ, so their formulas cannot be swapped. The 3-4-5 triangle has unequal legs and whole-number sides, the 45-45-90 triangle is isosceles with a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[a\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, and the 30-60-90 triangle has a hypotenuse twice its short leg. The <a href="/special-right-triangle-calculator/">special right triangle calculator</a> covers the 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 cases, while the <a href="/5-12-13-triangle-calculator/">5-12-13 triangle calculator</a> covers a different Pythagorean triple.</p>
  <p>The table below compares the three triangles side by side. Confirm the angles first, then pick the ratio that matches them: the wrong ratio gives a wrong hypotenuse and a wrong area.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3-4-5</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45-45-90</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30-60-90</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side ratio</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">3:4:5</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:1:√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1:√3:2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Angles</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">36.87°, 53.13°, 90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">45°, 45°, 90°</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">30°, 60°, 90°</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Equal legs</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Yes</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">No</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5k</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a√2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">2a</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Main property</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Pythagorean triple</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Isosceles right triangle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Special angle ratio</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Calculator focus</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scaling</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Special triangle calculations</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Special triangle calculations</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('three-four-five-triples.svg')} alt="Comparison of Pythagorean triples 3-4-5, 6-8-10, and 5-12-13, showing 6-8-10 is a scaled 3-4-5 while 5-12-13 is a different triple" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">6-8-10 is a scaled 3-4-5, while 5-12-13 is a different Pythagorean triple.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Using the 3-4-5 Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Fourteen errors show up again and again in 3-4-5 problems.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Multiplying only one side by the scale factor: with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> the sides are 6, 8, and 10, not 6, 4, and 5. Scale all three sides by the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to scale all three sides equally: scaling only the legs breaks the ratio and the right angle. Apply the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> to every side.</li>
    <li>Treating 5 as a leg instead of the hypotenuse: 5 is the longest side and sits opposite the 90° angle. <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5k]]')} /> is always the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Confusing the scale factor with a side length: <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is the multiplier, not a side. With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> the sides are 6, 8, and 10.</li>
    <li>Using zero as a valid scale factor: <span set:html={rich('[[k = 0]]')} /> makes all three sides zero, a degenerate figure, not a triangle. <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> must be greater than zero.</li>
    <li>Entering a negative scale factor: a negative <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> gives negative side lengths, which cannot describe a triangle. Enter a positive <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> only.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square k in the area formula: area grows with <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> squared. With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A = 6 \\times 9 = 54]]')} />, not 18.</li>
    <li>Confusing area with perimeter: area is in square units and perimeter is in linear units. With <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> both equal 24, but they measure different things.</li>
    <li>Adding only two sides when calculating perimeter: with <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[6 + 8 = 14]]')} /> leaves out the hypotenuse. Add all three sides.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: a leg in feet and a diagonal in inches cannot be combined directly. Convert every measurement to one unit first.</li>
    <li>Assuming that only 3, 4, and 5 work: any scaled copy, such as 6-8-10 or 9-12-15, is a valid 3-4-5 triangle.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every right triangle follows the 3:4:5 ratio: most right triangles have other ratios. Confirm the ratio before using the formulas.</li>
    <li>Confusing a 3-4-5 triangle with a 45-45-90 triangle: a 3-4-5 triangle has angles of about 36.87° and 53.13°, not two 45° angles.</li>
    <li>Rounding measurements unnecessarily: rounding early carries errors into later steps. Round only the final answer.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

