<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify a Scaled 3-4-5 Triangle</h2>
  <p>Four quick checks catch nearly every error.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Side Ratio</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a : b : c = 3 : 4 : 5]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[6 : 8 : 10]]')} /> divides by 2 to give <span set:html={rich('[[3 : 4 : 5]]')} />, so the check passes. If the sides do not reduce to 3:4:5, they are not a scaled 3-4-5 triangle.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[6^2 + 8^2 = 36 + 64 = 100]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[10^2 = 100]]')} />, so the theorem holds. A wrong value for any side breaks this equality.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Scale Factor Consistency</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{a}{3} = \\frac{b}{4} = \\frac{c}{5} = k]]')} />. Here each division gives 2, so <span set:html={rich('[[k = 2]]')} /> from all three sides. If the three divisions differ, at least one side is wrong.</p>
  <h3>Check 4: Side Ordering</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a < b < c]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[6 < 8 < 10]]')} />, so the ordering holds. If the supposed hypotenuse is not the longest side, the labels are mixed up.</p>
</section>

